    "q-80120": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80120",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-11,-8,-4)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $14x+8y-8z=-6.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-1$ and $y=-2$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14x+8y-8z & =-6 \\\\ 14(-1)+8(-2)-8z & =-6 \\\\ -8z & =24 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-1,-2,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,-2,-3⟩-⟨-11,-8,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10,6,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨14,8,-8⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|10\\cdot 14+6\\cdot 8+1\\cdot (-8)|}{\\sqrt{(14)^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-8)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|140+48-8|}{\\sqrt{196+64+64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,180\\,|}{\\sqrt{324}} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
